\begin{document}

\title{Spatiotemporal Deep Learning for IoT-Enabled Digital Health: From Predictive Models to Digital Twins and Emerging World Models}

\author{Xin~Xiang,~Hua~Fang,~Dengyi~Liu,~Ashikur~Nobel,~and~Honggang~Wang%
\thanks{The authors are with the Graduate Computer Science and Engineering Department, Katz School of Health and Science, Yeshiva University, New York, NY 10016 USA (e-mail: xxiang@mail.yu.edu; julia.fang@yu.edu; dliu6@mail.yu.edu; anobel@mail.yu.edu; honggang.wang@yu.edu).}}
%%
%% Sometimes the addresses are too long to fit on the page.  In this
%% case uncomment the lines below and fill them accodingly.
%%
%% Institute for Clarity in Documentation, P.O. Box 1212, Dublin,
%% Ohio, USA, 43017-6221}
%%
%%
%% Keywords. The author(s) should pick words that accurately describe
%% the work being presented. Separate the keywords with commas.

\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Health evolves jointly in time and space, yet clinical records sample it only intermittently. Connected health devices---wearables, continuous glucose monitors, and ambient environmental sensors---supply the missing context. The Internet of Things (IoT) is therefore the data layer on which digital health now depends: the deployed devices determine what is observed, at what rate, and where computation can run. This survey reviews deep spatiotemporal models for such connected health data under one organizing progression. Spatiotemporal models learn health dynamics; digital twins keep those dynamics synchronized with an individual patient; and world models extend the twin from prediction toward simulating candidate interventions. We categorize the spatiotemporal data types arising in connected digital health and compare recurrent, convolutional, graph, attention based, state space, and generative architectures by spatial handling, temporal handling, computational cost, and data requirements. We synthesize quantitative evidence from diabetes, depression, cardiac, and longitudinal clinical studies, together with their datasets, metrics, and reported performance, and note that dietary evidence remains qualitative. We then examine medical digital twins---multisource data fusion, federated learning under privacy constraints, and clinical visualization---and the emerging world model formulation, whose health applications remain largely conceptual rather than clinically evaluated. Across the surveyed work, spatial and environmental context often improves chronic disease prediction, whereas lifestyle factors stay underused and deployment characteristics are rarely reported. We close with challenges in data sparsity, heterogeneity, privacy, and continuous model updating, and with research directions for deployable connected health systems.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Spatiotemporal models, deep neural networks, digital health, Internet of Things, wearable sensing, longitudinal data, federated learning, digital twin, world models, chronic disease.
\end{IEEEkeywords}

