\fi
Connected devices are what make this joint view observable. In IoT-enabled digital health the sensing layer---wearables and implantables, continuous glucose monitors, ambient and environmental nodes, and smartphones acting as gateways---determines which physiological, behavioral, and contextual variables exist at all, at what sampling rate, with what gaps, and under which timestamp and calibration conventions. The network and edge layers then determine where a model may run and how fast it may respond: on the device, at a gateway, on a hospital edge server, or in the cloud under a federated protocol. Health data are therefore not modality neutral inputs but the output of a deployed system, and a spatiotemporal model chosen without reference to that system's acquisition and deployment conditions is unlikely to survive contact with it. This device to data relation is the thread we follow throughout the survey.

This survey argues that spatiotemporal modeling is a necessary step in this progression. Reviewing spatiotemporal models and their digital health applications, we clarify how environmental and lifestyle factors such as diet can enter longitudinal disease prediction, and how such models support faithful, personalized digital twins for healthcare.


We organize the survey around four research questions. \emph{RQ1}: which spatiotemporal data types arise in connected digital health, and how do sensing and deployment conditions constrain the model families that can consume them? \emph{RQ2}: what quantitative evidence exists that spatiotemporal deep learning improves clinical prediction across digital health domains, and how comparable is that evidence? \emph{RQ3}: what does a medical digital twin require of such models, and which of those requirements does current work actually meet? \emph{RQ4}: what distinguishes a world model based twin from adjacent formulations, and what evidence supports its clinical use? Our audience is twofold: developers who build spatiotemporal models and researchers who deploy them in digital health or digital twin settings. We introduce a practical taxonomy, examine application scenarios, recommend data processing pipelines and model choices, and give evaluation guidance. We then address deployment privacy concerns and solutions such as federated learning, and forecast future trends in digital twin based digital health. This is a narrative survey: we draw on peer-reviewed work from IEEE Xplore, PubMed, and Scopus together with recent preprints spanning roughly 2015--2026, prioritizing methods relevant to spatiotemporal deep learning in digital health. Table~\ref{tab:related-surveys} positions this survey against recent reviews spanning generic spatiotemporal data mining~\cite{wang2022deep,atluri2018stdm}, deep learning for health care~\cite{miotto2018deeplearning}, wearable and biotelemetry based digital health~\cite{Busnatu2022}, irregularly sampled longitudinal modeling~\cite{shukla2021irregular}, digital twins~\cite{Wang2023Survey,Sel2025VVUQ}, and the transfer of the twin idea to world models~\cite{Zheng2026DTtoWM}: none treats connected health acquisition, spatiotemporal model families, and twin synchronization together.

\emph{Our main contributions are as follows.} First, we develop a digital health taxonomy linking spatiotemporal data types (event, trajectory, point reference, raster, video), their preprocessing challenges (sparsity, irregular sampling, multi scale structure), the foundational architectures (CNN, GNN, RNN, Transformer), and clinical applications into a unified map from data to model to deployment. Second, we synthesize the major model families (CNN--RNN hybrids, spatiotemporal graph neural networks, Transformer based models, 3D-CNN and hybrid networks, and irregular time, longitudinal, and statistical deep learning approaches) with guidance on when each suits digital health data, illustrated across dietary, diabetes, depression, and cardiac applications. Third, we analyze deployment through medical digital twins, including privacy preserving federated learning, visualization, uncertainty quantification, and world model based patient simulators for counterfactual planning. Finally, we contribute an application and service level mapping for connected health: we relate connected health data characteristics (sensing modality, sampling regularity, spatial support, and subject coverage) and deployment conditions (where data are collected, where inference must run, and the bandwidth, latency, and privacy constraints of that placement) to spatiotemporal model choices, and identify the open challenges on the path to world model based digital twins.



For readers of IoT venues in particular, the practical question this survey answers is what can be built on top of a connected health deployment: which spatiotemporal model family a given sensing and inference placement regime can actually support, what such a model must report before it can be trusted at the edge or across federated sites, and where the published evidence stops and conceptual proposals begin.

The remainder is organized as follows. Section~\ref{sec:data} characterizes spatiotemporal data in digital health and its challenges (sparsity, irregular sampling, multi scale structure). Section~\ref{sec:history} reviews the development of deep learning based spatiotemporal methods. Section~\ref{sec:models} presents spatiotemporal models and their digital health applications across domains, including the longitudinal evidence. Section~\ref{sec:DT} covers integration within digital twin systems, including data collection and privacy. Section~\ref{sec:world_model_dt} develops world model based digital twins for patient state simulation and counterfactual planning. Section~\ref{sec:future} outlines future directions combining spatiotemporal modeling with digital twins.


\fi


\section{Survey Methodology}
\label{sec:method}
This is a \emph{narrative}, purposive survey, not a systematic review; Figure~\ref{fig:prisma} gives the flow and the counts.

We searched \textbf{IEEE Xplore}, \textbf{PubMed} and \textbf{Scopus}, extended by backward and forward citation chasing from the reviews in Table~\ref{tab:related-surveys}. Figure~\ref{fig:prisma} gives the search string, the 2015--2026 window and the search date; a fourth block (digital twin OR world model) served Sections~\ref{sec:DT} and~\ref{sec:world_model_dt}.

Eligibility and selection criteria were three. A record qualified for inclusion when it (i) modeled data indexed by both time and space, or longitudinal data with an explicit temporal model; (ii) addressed a clinical or public health task; and (iii) reported at least one quantitative result against a named baseline, which also served as the quality assessment. Exclusion followed from failing any one of them. A saturation rule governed retention: a study entered the synthesis only when it added a data type, model family, or application domain not already represented. Eight groups of records duplicated under different keys were merged in the reference list.

We ran the query against PubMed in September 2026 and logged the search results: 1{,}171 records on the spatiotemporal arm and 268 on the twin and world model arm, 3 on both, giving \textbf{1{,}436 unique records}. Removing 36 non-research item types and 194 reviews left \textbf{1{,}206 records screened} on title and abstract. We applied criteria (i)--(iii) as explicit textual rules, so the decision is reproducible: 99 records were excluded as not a human clinical or public health task, 127 for carrying neither a space--time nor a longitudinal structure, and 617 for reporting no quantitative result against a named alternative, leaving \textbf{363 rule-eligible records}. We re-read a random 60 decisions by hand: 54 agreed, and of the 21 called eligible 15 were confirmed, a precision of 71\% (95\% CI 50--86\%). Every disagreement was an over-inclusion, so the pool is better estimated at about \textbf{260 studies (180--310)}. Two limits apply. The rule judges abstracts, so it under-counts studies whose abstracts omit results; and only PubMed was count-logged, being the one source of the three with a programmatic interface, so IEEE Xplore, Scopus and citation chasing contributed records outside these counts.

From the pool to the twelve studies of Table~\ref{tab:evidence} is a purposive step, not a ranking: we sampled them to span five data types and four domains, and only one of the 363 is cited here. The query recovers 6 of our 12; the rest are indexed elsewhere or carry no spatial term to match, the property that made the longitudinal studies worth including.

