\fi

\paragraph{Versatility.} All four domains pair CNN or GCN spatial encoders with temporal modules (LSTM/GRU/Transformer) or STGCN to jointly model space and time across video, raster, and EEG data~\cite{2,3,4,6}, differing mainly in priors---anatomical connectivity for depression~\cite{4}, physical equations for cardiac flow and electrophysiology~\cite{5,7}.

\paragraph{Small samples, high dimensions.} Neuroimaging and 4D cardiac data are high dimensional but small in sample due to cost and ethics, risking overfitting and poor transfer~\cite{4,5,6}; physical priors~\cite{7}, active learning, federated learning, and self-supervised attention to salient regions help~\cite{2,4}.

\paragraph{Multimodal fusion.} Integrating environmental, behavioral, and genetic data with the primary signals yields richer assessments---glucose plus geography for diabetes risk maps~\cite{XiaoLi2016DiabetesSpatiotemporal}, physiological or physical priors for cardiac interpretability~\cite{5,7}. Statistics research reinforces this: convection--diffusion and dynamic deformation models encode physical transport and nonstationary dependence, and disease mapping and spatial SIR emulators link exposure, regional outcomes, and temporal dynamics through Bayesian hierarchies~\cite{liu2022convectionDiffusion,castroMorales2025dynamicDeformation,wang2024respiratoryDiseaseMapping,trostle2024spatialSIR,wikle2023statisticalDeepLearning}.

\paragraph{Deployment and efficiency.} Macro scale regional forecasting must incorporate socioeconomic, transport, and mobility factors, where adjacency uncertainty and noise hamper robustness~\cite{2,3}. Rising data resolution raises compute cost~\cite{5,7}, motivating compression, edge computing, and hardware-software co-design for real-time monitoring.

\paragraph{Explainability and standardization.} Clinical use demands outputs a clinician can inspect, and the reviewed studies most often supply attention maps and salient space--time segments~\cite{2,4}. Attention weights should not, however, be equated with an explanation of the decision. Jain and Wallace showed that attention distributions can often be replaced by substantially different ones without changing the prediction~\cite{jain2019attention}; Serrano and Smith found that the highest weighted components are frequently not those whose removal changes the output~\cite{serrano2019attention}; and Wiegreffe and Pinter, while defending a weaker reading, agree that attention alone does not establish faithfulness~\cite{wiegreffe2019attention}. The difficulty compounds in the multi-layer, multi-head spatiotemporal encoders used here, where weights are aggregated across layers, heads, space, and time before being displayed. What clinicians ask for is closer to contextual, actionable justification than to a saliency image~\cite{tonekaboni2019clinicians}, and for high stakes decisions an inherently interpretable model may be preferable to a post-hoc rationalization of an opaque one~\cite{rudin2019stopexplaining}. Attention maps are therefore best treated as a debugging and hypothesis generating aid, with faithfulness testing---perturbation or ablation of the attended region and agreement with mechanistic knowledge---as the evaluation step this literature still lacks. Separately, inconsistent annotation across centers limits reproducibility~\cite{6}, calling for data governance and benchmarking.

\fi

\subsection{Longitudinal Evidence within the Taxonomy}
\label{sec:longitudinal}
\ifsubmission
Three studies in Table~\ref{tab:evidence} have no spatial component, and we include them deliberately: the twin argument of Sections~\ref{sec:DT} and~\ref{sec:world_model_dt} rests on \emph{synchronization}---that a virtual representation can be repeatedly updated from new observations of one individual and re-issue a prediction---and they are among the clearest evidence in the reviewed sample that dynamic re-prediction from accumulating history outperforms one-shot prediction, which no spatially explicit study tests. Lin and Luo~\cite{lin2022deep} treat each visit as a token with masked self-attention; Nitski et al.~\cite{nitski2021long} find a Transformer best for 1- and 5-year mortality, externally validated on a second registry; Glaser et al.~\cite{glaser2022deep} show sequence models over serial DXA outperforming single-scan models. In Table~\ref{tab:evidence} the IoT layer and the spatial and temporal structure are our classification of the data each study uses, not stated by the study; no quantitative dietary or lifestyle study was surfaced by our search under the stated criteria.

None of the three uses a continuously sensed stream; all draw on episodic encounters, which is what makes them informative here: they establish the value of updating a prediction as history accumulates while sampling it a few times a year. A connected deployment changes the sampling regime rather than the model class, since a visit token Transformer accepts wearable derived weekly summaries alongside laboratory panels. Serial DXA in particular is not a health-IoT modality: the scans are intermittently acquired clinical imaging obtained at visits years apart, so the re-synchronization interval rather than the architecture separates this setting from the wearable case.
\else
Complementing the irregular-time and longitudinal \emph{methods} of Section~\ref{sec:history}, this section reviews clinical \emph{studies} that apply DNN based medical and longitudinal models to two tasks---dynamic prediction and risk stratification. These studies leverage longitudinal data, from multivariate clinical measurements to sequential imaging, using multivariate functional principal component analysis (MFPCA), CNNs, Transformers, and recurrent networks for survival prediction, mortality risk, and personalized care. Table~\ref{tab:evidence} synthesizes representative studies across the four application domains and these longitudinal tasks, with their datasets, evaluation metrics, and reported performance; a recurring theme is that attention based models tend to outperform recurrent and convolutional baselines once follow-up data are dynamically incorporated, though reported gains vary and several studies rely on small or private cohorts.

\emph{Why longitudinal-only studies belong in a spatiotemporal survey.} Three studies below---Lin and Luo~\cite{lin2022deep}, Nitski et al.~\cite{nitski2021long}, and Glaser et al.~\cite{glaser2022deep}---have no spatial component. We include them deliberately. The digital twin argument of Sections~\ref{sec:DT} and~\ref{sec:world_model_dt} rests on a claim about \emph{synchronization}: that a virtual representation can be repeatedly updated from new observations of the same individual and re-issue a prediction. These studies are the strongest evidence we found that dynamic re-prediction from accumulating history outperforms one-shot prediction, which is the temporal half of the twin mechanism, and none of the spatially explicit studies above tests that property. Our eligibility bar here is correspondingly narrow: repeated observations of the same subject, an outcome defined at a later time, and an explicit comparison against a model that ignores follow-up. The division of labor with Section~\ref{sec:history} is that the earlier section covers longitudinal \emph{methods}---how irregular sampling, decay, and missingness are represented---while this section covers longitudinal \emph{evidence}, namely whether such models change a clinical estimate; architectures introduced there are cross-referenced rather than re-described.

The selection protocol is given in Section~\ref{sec:method}.

\emph{Where connected health data would enter.} None of these three studies uses a wearable or any continuously sensed stream; all draw on episodic clinical encounters. That is what makes them informative for an IoT reading: they establish the value of updating a prediction as history accumulates while sampling that history only a few times a year. A connected deployment changes the sampling regime rather than the model class---the visit token Transformer of Lin and Luo, or the per-visit embedding followed by recurrence of Glaser et al., accepts a denser stream of wearable derived summaries such as weekly activity, sleep regularity, resting heart rate, or glycemic variability in place of, or interleaved with, laboratory panels. The questions this raises are about the sampling regime: whether between-visit wearable summaries add predictive information over the clinical trajectory, how a dense noisy stream should be weighted against a sparse reliable one, and how often the model must be re-run and its update transmitted to remain useful. None of the three addresses these, and we found no longitudinal risk model in this literature that ingests a continuous consumer sensing stream.


\subsection{Deep Learning for Dynamic Prediction of Multivariate Longitudinal and Survival Data}
Lin et al.~\cite{lin2022deep} jointly model multivariate longitudinal data and time-to-event outcomes, avoiding the restrictive assumptions of classical joint models. They compare MFPCA based survival models (MFPCA-Cox, MFPCA-DeepSurv), efficient but limited in nonlinear interactions; MATCH-net, a CNN handling irregular sampling and missingness but predicting survival without forecasting trajectories; and TransformerJM, which treats each visit as a token and uses masked self-attention to jointly predict future measurements and update survival probabilities, excelling under nonlinear interactions and non-proportional hazards.

\subsection{Deep Learning Approaches for Long-Term Mortality Risk Stratification in Liver Transplant Recipients}

Nitski et al.~\cite{nitski2021long} forecast 1-year and 5-year mortality in liver transplant recipients from longitudinal clinical data, replacing static logistic regression that ignores follow-up dynamics. Among multilayer perceptron (MLP), RNN, temporal convolutional network (TCN), and Transformer architectures, the Transformer achieved the highest area under the ROC curve (AUROC); trained on the Scientific Registry of Transplant Recipients (SRTR) and externally validated on University Health Network (UHN) data with transfer learning, it enables continually updated, real-time risk stratification.


\subsection{Deep Learning for All-Cause Mortality Prediction from Longitudinal DXA Imaging}

Glaser et al.~\cite{glaser2022deep} predict all-cause mortality from longitudinal total-body dual-energy X-ray absorptiometry (DXA) imaging, showing that deep networks extract predictive features directly from raw scans (alone or with clinical risk factors) and that sequence models over multiple scans outperform single-scan models. They use single-record models (a DenseNet121 over image crops, a fully connected network over clinical metadata, and a combined-modality fusion) and sequence models that feed per visit embeddings to an LSTM to capture the temporal evolution of body composition. Serial DXA should not be read as a health-IoT modality: the scans are intermittently acquired clinical imaging, obtained at scheduled study visits years apart, in a facility, by a technologist, so the series is longitudinal in the epidemiological sense rather than a sensor feed. Treating it as a continuous stream would overstate how often a twin built on such data could be re-synchronized, and the re-synchronization interval, not the architecture, is what separates this setting from the wearable case.


\fi

