\section{Spatiotemporal Models in Medical Digital Twins}
\label{sec:DT}
\ifsubmission
The twin layer at the foot of Figure~\ref{fig:taxonomy} is where the pipeline closes. Doing so faces three constraints: data that are insufficient and heterogeneous, security and privacy, and presentation to clinicians and patients. A digital twin addresses them as a cyber-physical feedback loop in which multimodal data from sensors and IoT devices stream to a virtual replica, are analyzed, and feed back to the physical side; in health care it is described in four tiers from visualization through prediction and optimization to closed loop control~\cite{Tao2018,NIST2021}. Faruqui \emph{et al.}\ paired a transfer learned network with a clinician guided controller to generate daily lifestyle recommendations for type 2 diabetes~\cite{Faruqui2024}.

\emph{Fusion and its limits.} Fusion is not monotonically beneficial, and the reviewed studies rarely say when it fails. An added modality contributes noise when its signal-to-noise ratio is low relative to the primary channel; bias when availability differs across subgroups, so a twin fusing a wearable stream inherits the demographics of who wears the device; cost linear in devices and bandwidth; and failure points, since a fused model is only as available as its least reliable input unless trained for modality dropout. The test we would ask is whether a fused twin still meets its stated accuracy with each modality withheld in turn; it is not reported.

\emph{Human factors.} Post-hoc attribution reports what a model weighted without telling a clinician whether to act~\cite{rudin2019stopexplaining,tonekaboni2019clinicians}. A twin needs an explicit account of who intervenes and where: a person should adjudicate identity resolution at intake, approve any re-synchronization that materially shifts a risk stratum, read any output that changes therapy, and review subgroup calibration and fairness at audit---most urgently where uncertainty is high, the recommendation contradicts mechanistic expectation, or the patient falls outside the training cohort's support.

\emph{Privacy and federated learning.} Healthcare data are a frequent target of fraud and ransomware~\cite{Seh2020HealthcareDataBreaches}, motivating training without moving raw data. Federated learning sends a global model to institutional clients that train locally and return encrypted updates, at the cost of communication overhead, non-IID data, and adversarial exposure~\cite{prayitno2021FLhealthcare,aouedi2022FLmedical}. Results are nevertheless usually a single accuracy figure against a centralized model, which cannot establish deployability. A federated spatiotemporal twin should report client availability and participation, the degree of non-IID and device shift, rounds to convergence, update size and total bandwidth, round latency, client energy and memory ceilings, treatment of stragglers and asynchrony, personalization back to each site, an attack and defense evaluation covering gradient inversion, membership inference and update poisoning, the differential privacy budget with its accuracy cost, and comparison against both centralized and local-only baselines. Device-layer authentication and encryption of the link secure what the model layer cannot. We give this as a checklist because the reviewed studies do not supply the fields.

\emph{Rendering is not synchronization.} Extended-reality rendering of anatomy or data~\cite{Auvinet2018AR} is properly judged on comprehension and satisfaction; the improvement reported by Louis et al.~\cite{Louis2021VR} is evidence of that kind, about the interface rather than the model behind it. A synchronized twin interface, bound to a continuously updated patient state, is properly judged on clinical endpoints: calibration of the displayed risk, agreement with the patient's subsequent course, and whether the clinician's decision changes and improves outcome. Most visualization work surveyed here is of the first kind.
\else
Bringing spatiotemporal models into clinical workflows faces three constraints along the symptom--visit--diagnosis--treatment--rehabilitation pathway: (1) insufficient and heterogeneous data, since reliance on recall and self-report creates gaps and inconsistent time labels while constitution, lifestyle, and environment add heterogeneity that no single variable explains; (2) data security and privacy, balancing the use of highly sensitive health information against compliance requirements; and (3) presentation and interpretation, communicating outputs to clinicians and patients across diverse, interdependent data types.

Digital twins address these jointly by building a virtual ``human mirror'' linked to the individual in near real time, aggregating and governing multisource time series from wearables and fixed medical devices with cloud based standardization, temporal alignment, and cross-modal fusion. Population scale longitudinal data improve predictive accuracy and generalization; federated learning enables training under data remain in place constraints; and multidimensional visualization with human in the loop mechanisms present predictions interpretably, closing the loop between decision support and self-management. We next discuss data acquisition and fusion, privacy preserving modeling, and clinical visualization.

\subsection{Digital Twin in Digital Health}

A digital twin (DT) is a cyber-physical feedback loop in which real-time multimodal data from sensors and IoT devices stream to a high fidelity virtual replica, are analyzed, and feed back to the physical asset. Originating in aerospace and manufacturing for full life cycle support~\cite{Tao2018}, in healthcare it emphasizes runtime, with four tiers---visualization and monitoring, prediction, optimization, and closed loop control~\cite{NIST2021,sun2023digitaltwin}. Early work formalized the healthcare digital twin as a virtual patient built from biological and omics data, promising precision medicine while raising ethical questions~\cite{Bruynseels2018DigitalTwins}; at the tissue scale, cell atlas efforts such as the NIH Human Biomolecular Atlas Program (HuBMAP), launched in 2018, map the body at cellular resolution to parameterize a digital twin of the healthy human~\cite{HuBMAP2019}. The most advanced form is the personal digital twin~\cite{deKerckhove2021}.

Personal twins fuse continuous glucose monitors, wearables, home IoT, and genomics into patient specific models. Faruqui \emph{et al.}\ applied transfer learning to a neural network and paired it with a clinician guided feedback controller to generate daily, personalized lifestyle recommendations for managing type 2 diabetes; prediction accuracy exceeded eighty percent and produced sustained improvements in HbA1c and weight~\cite{Faruqui2024}. Silfvergren \emph{et al.} built a multi-organ metabolic model that personalises fasting schedules and macronutrient ratios by simulating hepatic glycogen turnover \cite{Silfvergren2022}. For cardiovascular care, continuous PPG and ECG streams injected into cardiac twins detect atrial fibrillation and impending de-compensation, reducing readmissions \cite{Bayoumy2021,Williams2023}. Because personal twins rely on high frequency, privacy sensitive data, they are increasingly paired with federated learning and differential privacy.



\subsection{Challenges in Data Collection and Prediction for Medical Digital Twins}

In the construction of medical digital twins, data collection is both the most fundamental and one of the most challenging stages. The main difficulty arises from the diversity of data sources. In medical contexts, data originate from electronic health records (EHR), medical imaging (CT, MRI, ultrasound), laboratory test results, physiological signals captured by wearable devices, and real-time monitoring data from ICU systems. These different sources exhibit significant disparities in sparsity and sampling rates: for example, EHR records are sparse and infrequent, whereas ICU monitoring devices capture data at millisecond or second-level granularity; imaging data are usually interval based snapshots that fail to fully capture dynamic processes. Such heterogeneity leads to inconsistencies across temporal, spatial, and semantic dimensions, thereby increasing the difficulty of data alignment and integration.

Moreover, there are substantial differences between modalities, such as textual medical records versus imaging data, or structured laboratory indicators versus continuous physiological signals. Addressing the integration of heterogeneous multimodal data remains a key challenge in the implementation of digital twins in healthcare. Previous studies have pointed out that the standardization and fusion of multi-source heterogeneous medical data are prerequisites for achieving accurate digital twins\cite{tao2019digital,reddy2015healthcare}.

Multimodal fusion is central to the prediction stage, since single modality data (e.g., ECG alone) are noise and gap prone; combining physiological signals, laboratory results, and imaging better simulates progression, with cross-modal alignment mitigating sparsity and single-point failures~\cite{baltrusaitis2019multimodal}, and deep multimodal fusion outperforming unimodal models in cardiovascular prediction~\cite{huang2020fusion}.



\emph{When fusion does not help.} Fusion is not monotonically beneficial, and the reviewed studies rarely state the conditions under which it fails. Adding a modality adds noise when its signal-to-noise ratio is low relative to the primary channel, so a weak stream dilutes a strong one unless the fusion is gated or weighted. It adds bias when modalities differ in availability across subgroups: a twin that fuses a wearable stream inherits the demographics of who owns and wears the device, and a model trained on complete cases silently conditions on that availability. It adds cost linearly in devices, storage, and bandwidth, and superlinearly in calibration and alignment effort. It also adds failure points, because a fused model in deployment is only as available as its least reliable input: a modality missing at inference time, whether from a flat battery, a disconnected sensor, or an unperformed scan, degrades the prediction unless the architecture is explicitly trained for modality dropout. The test we would ask of any fused twin is whether it still meets its stated accuracy with each modality withheld in turn, and whether an added modality earns its marginal cost. We found neither reported in the studies surveyed here.

Patient heterogeneity complicates prediction: variability from demographics, genetics, environment, disease subtypes, and individual trajectories means identical indicators (e.g., the same BMI or blood pressure) can imply different risks across populations, and ignoring it introduces systematic bias~\cite{obermeyer2016predicting,rajkomar2019machine,yu2018artificial}. Incorporating individual priors---baseline characteristics, genetics, history---into patient specific twins, with Bayesian or meta learning for fast adaptation from little data, improves specificity and robustness~\cite{bjornsson2020digitaltwins}.

Heterogeneity also harms interpretability: complex decision boundaries create a black box effect that undermines clinical trust~\cite{rudin2019stopexplaining}. Since interpretability is as important as accuracy in high risk care~\cite{tonekaboni2019clinicians,lundberg2017unified,voigt2021digital}, twins increasingly use attention based feature attribution, post hoc explanations (SHAP, LIME), and causality driven models with medical knowledge graphs~\cite{ahmad2018interpretable}.

\emph{Human factors, not only post-hoc explanation.} Post-hoc attribution reports what the model weighted; it does not tell a clinician whether to act, and comprehension is a property of the reader rather than of the saliency map. A twin therefore needs an explicit account of who intervenes, where, and on what evidence. At data intake a person should adjudicate identity resolution, device provenance, and implausible values that automated range checks admit. At model update a person should approve any re-synchronization that materially shifts a patient's risk stratum, since silent drift is otherwise invisible. At inference a person should be the mandatory reader of any output that changes therapy, with the twin ranking options rather than selecting one. At audit a person should review the subgroup calibration that determines whether the twin is equally trustworthy across the population it serves. The cases that most need routing to a human are those in which the twin's own uncertainty is high, the recommendation contradicts a mechanistic expectation, the input stream has recently changed regime through a new device, a firmware update, or a care transition, or the patient falls outside the demographic support of the training cohort. Comprehension outcomes---whether clinicians and patients read the twin's uncertainty correctly---belong alongside accuracy and are currently absent from this literature.

\subsection{Medical Data Privacy Problem and Federated Learning in Digital Twin}

\emph{Why medical data privacy is critical.} Healthcare data are highly sensitive and valuable on the black market, making the sector a frequent target of identity theft, fraud, and ransomware; a single healthcare breach costs on average about USD 10.1 million, and the 2015 Anthem breach alone exposed nearly 80 million records~\cite{Seh2020HealthcareDataBreaches,Argaw2020HospitalCybersecurity,Nemec2024HealthcareSecurity}. Centralized storage is especially vulnerable, motivating privacy enhancing methods such as blockchain, differential privacy, and federated learning as secure alternatives~\cite{rieke2020digitalhealth,dhiman2022FL}.
\ifsubmission\else
\begin{figure*}[t] \centering \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{Fig5_redrawn_600dpi.png} \caption{The federated learning training cycle: a global model is distributed to participating institutions, each trains it locally on its own private data, and only the resulting model updates are returned to a central server for aggregation, so raw patient data never leave the institution.} \Description{A schematic of the federated learning process, showing local training on multiple clients and global aggregation of model updates on a central server without sharing raw data.} \label{fig:Federated Learning Process} \end{figure*}
\fi


\emph{How federated learning works.} FL trains a shared model across institutions without moving raw data\ifsubmission\else{} (Figure~\ref{fig:Federated Learning Process})\fi : a global model is initialized and sent to clients, each trains locally and returns only encrypted updates, which are aggregated and redistributed iteratively, aiding HIPAA and GDPR compliance. Challenges include communication overhead, non-IID data, and adversarial robustness~\cite{prayitno2021FLhealthcare,coelho2023FLsecurity}.


\emph{Federated learning for spatiotemporal models in digital twins.} Combining FL with spatiotemporal longitudinal models inside a digital twin yields a privacy preserving pipeline: local twins on edge or hospital servers encode multivariate trajectories (diet logs, CGM traces, regional lifestyle factors) into latent representations, refine a shared network on private cohorts, and send only encrypted updates to an aggregator that synchronizes thousands of twins in compliance with data protection rules~\cite{aouedi2022FLmedical}\ifsubmission\else{} (Fig.~\ref{fig:architecture of FL})\fi . This enhances personalized optimization and real-time monitoring while letting each twin learn from a larger virtual cohort, valuable in nutrition centric applications where dietary patterns vary by geography yet privacy rules block sharing~\cite{nguyen2022FLsmarthealthcare}. A twin aware stack adds a semantic alignment layer harmonizing site schemas and a feedback layer streaming uncertainties to bedside dashboards; with transformer based encoders, such designs aim to lower glycemic prediction error while reducing communication overhead, though quantitative evidence remains preliminary.

\emph{What a federated evaluation must report.} Federated results in this literature are usually summarized by a single accuracy figure set against a centralized model, which is not enough to judge deployability. A federated spatiotemporal twin should report, at minimum: client availability and per-round participation; the degree and kind of non-IID heterogeneity, including site and device shift; communication rounds to convergence; update size per round and total bandwidth consumed; wall-clock round latency; energy drawn on the client; client compute and memory ceilings; the treatment of stragglers and dropout, and whether operation is synchronous or asynchronous; whether and how the global model is personalized back to each site; the attack and defense evaluation performed, covering at least gradient inversion, membership inference, and update poisoning; the privacy budget where differential privacy is applied, together with the accuracy cost at that budget; and comparison against both a centralized and a local-only baseline, since a federated system that does not improve on local training does not justify its coordination cost. We give these as an evaluation checklist rather than as a synthesis, because the studies reviewed here do not supply the fields.

\emph{Outlook.} Converging edge--cloud FL, explainable spatiotemporal models, and physics informed organ simulators could push digital twins toward closed loop companions that propose, simulate, and verify lifestyle or medication changes before any real action, requiring standardized ontologies, differential privacy for gradient sharing, and lightweight continual learning on wearable class hardware. 
\ifsubmission\else
\begin{figure*}[t] \centering \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{Fig6_redrawn_600dpi.png} \caption{A general architecture for federated learning of spatiotemporal models in digital health: distributed institutions encode local spatiotemporal patient data, train a shared model on their private cohorts, and exchange only encrypted updates with a central aggregator that synchronizes the per-patient digital twins.} \Description{A general architecture of federated learning for spatiotemporal models in digital health, showing distributed data sources and collaborative training without sharing raw data.} \label{fig:architecture of FL} \end{figure*}
\fi

\subsection{Visualization in Digital Twin}

Digital twins enable dynamic, personalized visualization. In surgery, computer generated vitals on a feedback screen improve ergonomics~\cite{Auvinet2018AR}, and with extended reality they overlay operative regions and incision lines for precise cuts~\cite{QUESADAOLARTE20221580}. AI powered twins generate live, data driven graphical analysis for the operating room and routine check-ups~\cite{Vallee2023DT}, while AR display of CT scans supports preplanning~\cite{Southworth2020XR}; preoperative 3D mapping with marked incisions has been shown to shorten surgery time~\cite{Auvinet2018AR}.

For patients, xR presentations aid comprehension: virtual brain models for doctor--patient communication raised satisfaction about 25\% above the average of the HCAHPS standardized patient-experience survey across 257 patients~\cite{Louis2021VR}.

\emph{Rendering is not synchronization.} Two different things are described as twin visualization in this literature, and they should be judged against different evidence. The first is three-dimensional or extended-reality rendering of patient anatomy or data: a virtual brain model used to explain a procedure, or an overlay guiding an incision. Its proper endpoints are comprehension, communication, workload, and satisfaction; the satisfaction improvement reported by Louis et al.~\cite{Louis2021VR} is evidence of exactly this kind, and it is a genuine result about the interface rather than about the model behind it. The second is a synchronized twin interface, in which the display is bound to a continuously updated patient state and changes as new observations arrive. Its proper endpoints are clinical: calibration of the displayed risk, agreement with the patient's subsequent course, whether the clinician's decision changes, and whether that change improves outcome. A high satisfaction score for a rendering says nothing about the validity of a synchronized twin, and a well calibrated twin behind an unreadable interface will not be used. Most of the visualization work surveyed here is of the first kind, and we are not aware of a study evaluating a synchronized health twin interface against clinical endpoints.

Twins also visualize dietary health over time; Gkouskou et al.~\cite{gkouskou2020virtual} propose lifelong nutritional snapshots integrating metabolomic, immune, behavioral, and gut microbial parameters, feeding a longitudinal spatiotemporal model of nutritional health.

\fi

\section{World Model Based Digital Twins in Digital Health}
\label{sec:world_model_dt}
\ifsubmission
This section is a \emph{proposed conceptual framework and research agenda}, not a synthesis of evaluated clinical systems: among the studies reviewed above we found no health digital twin that learns an action conditioned latent transition and has been validated against patient outcomes. We state what such a system would require and mark open problems as open.

Two criteria separate the adjacent concepts, and without them a survey risks renaming ordinary latent trajectory prediction as a world model and ordinary repeated prediction as a twin. A model is a \emph{world model} only when its transition is conditioned on an action, so that a rollout answers what would happen under an intervention: an encoder, latent state, transition and decoder without an action model give a state-space or disease progression model, adding a reward and planner gives model-based reinforcement learning, a mechanistic simulator specifies its transition from theory rather than learning it, and a causal model answers interventional queries under identification assumptions. A system is a \emph{digital twin} only when it is bound to one identified individual and kept synchronized with a path back to that individual; one-way updating without that return path is a digital shadow. On these criteria the systems reviewed in Section~\ref{sec:DT} are predominantly shadows with predictive heads. Such a system would roll a latent patient state forward under candidate actions across three layers---representation, synchronization, and action conditioned rollout with human review~\cite{Sadee2025MedicalDT}---and must be evaluated as a coupled pipeline rather than as a model.

Active simulation widens the attack surface beyond the confidentiality concerns of Section~\ref{sec:DT}: a compromised predictive twin produces a wrong number, a compromised world model twin a wrong plan, and where the loop actuates, a wrong action. Sensor spoofing drives the latent state to an attacker chosen region that seeds every rollout; action label tampering inverts the estimated effect of an intervention while leaving predictive accuracy intact; state poisoning through malicious federated updates shifts the learned dynamics rather than a decision boundary; model extraction and privacy leakage from released rollouts expose an identified patient's state; and planner manipulation makes an unsafe trajectory appear optimal. Controls map onto the same layers: per-device authentication, provenance for observations and action labels, signed and reversible updates with anomaly screening of client contributions, a privacy budget on released rollouts, an audit log binding each recommendation to a model version, and a safety filter rejecting trajectories that violate physiological or protocol constraints.

Validation of such a system cannot rest on retrospective comparison with held-out histories, because most interventions are counterfactual; it should add calibration across horizons, subgroup analysis, external cohorts, and prospective study. Uncertainty grows over long rollouts, so a usable system reports calibrated intervals, separates aleatoric from epistemic uncertainty, detects out-of-distribution states, and signals when no reliable recommendation is possible~\cite{Sel2025VVUQ}.
\else
This section is a \emph{proposed conceptual framework and research agenda}, not a synthesis of evaluated clinical systems. Among the primary studies reviewed above we found no health digital twin that learns an action conditioned latent transition and has been validated against patient outcomes. We therefore state what such a system would require and mark open problems as open, rather than describing capabilities as established.

A world model, in the sense used here, learns a compact latent state together with a transition that can be rolled forward under hypothetical actions, in contrast to a conventional spatiotemporal model that maps observed history to a future outcome. Coupled to a digital twin, such a model would compress multimodal observations (EHR, wearables, imaging, mobility, exposures, lifestyle) into an evolving patient state and produce trajectories under alternative interventions, joining representation learning, personalized twins, and decision planning. The move from digital twins to world models has so far been articulated for mobile edge general intelligence rather than for clinical care~\cite{Zheng2026DTtoWM}; we are not aware of a corresponding clinical evaluation.

\emph{Distinguishing the adjacent concepts.} The terms in this area overlap, and without an explicit comparison a survey risks renaming ordinary latent trajectory prediction as a world model and ordinary repeated prediction as a digital twin. \ifsubmission Two criteria do most of the work.\else Table~\ref{tab:wm-taxonomy} states the components we take to be necessary and optional for each. Two distinctions do most of the work.\fi A model becomes a \emph{world model} only when its transition is conditioned on an action, so that a rollout answers what would happen under an intervention rather than what will happen next; a latent state and a transition alone give a state-space model. A system becomes a \emph{digital twin} only when it is bound to one identified instance and kept synchronized with it, with some path back to that instance; automatic one-way updating without that return path is a digital shadow, and a model that is simply re-run on new data is neither. On these criteria, the health systems reviewed in Section~\ref{sec:DT} are predominantly shadows with predictive heads, and the world model layer described below remains a proposal.

