\fi

\subsection{Patient-State Simulation and Counterfactual Planning}
A health world model would simulate patient-state evolution rather than predict a single endpoint: given the current latent state and an action (medication change, diet, rehabilitation, sleep, exposure reduction), it produces a short or long horizon trajectory of physiological, behavioral, or clinical variables. Such a counterfactual capability would be valuable in chronic disease management, where clinicians compare intervention paths before choosing; whether it can be delivered at clinically useful accuracy is untested.

Unlike generic generative models, these twins stay anchored to a specific patient: the twin supplies continual synchronization as new observations update the latent state and constrain rollouts, while spatiotemporal learning supplies the representation backbone encoding temporal dynamics and spatial context (regional exposures, organ imaging, mobility, behavior). Together they would move from passive monitoring toward active scenario testing, with clinicians retaining final interpretation.

\subsection{Layered Architecture for Spatiotemporal World Models}
An integrated architecture would have three layers: spatiotemporal representation models that embed multimodal longitudinal data (physiological series, mobility, imaging, diet, exposures); a digital twin layer maintaining synchronization through continual updating and patient calibration; and a world model layer generating action conditioned rollouts on the twin's latent state, extending the twin from monitoring into simulation and planning with human in the loop review~\cite{Sadee2025MedicalDT}.

This structure shows world models are not isolated modules: their reliability depends on upstream multimodal fusion, temporal alignment, missing data handling, and personalization, and their usability on downstream interfaces conveying assumptions, trajectories, and uncertainty. A world model based twin should thus be evaluated as a coupled system spanning ingestion, representation, simulation, uncertainty, visualization, and workflow integration.

\subsection{Attack Surface of a World Model Based Twin}
Active simulation and planning widen the attack surface beyond the data confidentiality concerns of Section~\ref{sec:DT}. A predictive twin that is compromised produces a wrong number; a world model twin that is compromised produces a wrong \emph{plan}, and if any part of the loop actuates, a wrong action. The threats specific to this setting attach to identifiable points of the layered architecture above.

At the sensing layer, \emph{sensor spoofing} injects fabricated or replayed measurements so the latent state is driven to an attacker chosen region, which is more damaging here than in a monitoring system because the corrupted state seeds every rollout. At the ingestion layer, \emph{action label tampering} corrupts the record of which intervention was administered; since the transition is action conditioned, mislabeled actions systematically invert the model's estimate of what an intervention does, and the damage is invisible in ordinary predictive accuracy. At the training and update layer, \emph{state poisoning} through malicious client updates in a federated deployment shifts the learned dynamics rather than just the decision boundary. At the interface layer, \emph{model extraction} recovers a usable copy of the twin from query access, and \emph{privacy leakage from trajectories and rollouts} is a distinct exposure: a simulated trajectory is derived from one identified patient's state, so releasing rollouts can disclose attributes that were never released as raw data. Where a language or agentic interface mediates access, \emph{adversarial interface inputs} may steer the planner, and \emph{planner manipulation} more generally covers reward or objective tampering that makes an unsafe trajectory appear optimal. Finally, \emph{unsafe actuation} is the terminal risk wherever the twin is permitted to close the loop onto a pump, stimulator, or automated care pathway.

The corresponding controls map onto the same layers: device and gateway authentication with per-device keys at the sensing layer; integrity protection and provenance for observations and action labels at ingestion; signed, verifiable, and reversible model updates with anomaly screening of client contributions at the update layer; rate limiting and output perturbation against extraction, and a privacy budget applied to released rollouts rather than only to training, at the interface; an append-only audit log binding every issued recommendation to the model version and the observations that produced it; and a safety filter between planner and actuator that rejects trajectories violating physiological or protocol constraints regardless of predicted reward. None of the studies reviewed here evaluates these controls for a health world model twin, and we treat that as a prerequisite for any deployment that acts rather than merely advises.

\subsection{Verification, Validation, and Uncertainty}
Verification, validation, and uncertainty quantification are critical because simulated trajectories can influence real decisions. Verification checks that the implementation behaves as intended: pipelines preserve temporal order, action labels map to meaningful interventions, constraints prevent impossible states, and rollouts are reproducible, with stress tests for missing values, delayed or corrupted streams, and distribution shift across sites.

Validation checks clinical credibility. Retrospective comparison with held-out histories is insufficient because most interventions are counterfactual, so validation should add calibration across horizons, subgroup analysis, comparison with mechanistic knowledge, external cohorts, and prospective studies, assessing whether the model ranks interventions consistently with evidence, flags unsafe trajectories, and stays stable under input perturbations.

Uncertainty grows over long rollouts through error accumulation, hidden confounding, partial observability, and multiple plausible futures. A useful system reports calibrated intervals, separates aleatoric from epistemic uncertainty, detects out-of-distribution states, and signals when no reliable recommendation is possible---safeguards essential for trusted decision support rather than opaque simulators~\cite{Sel2025VVUQ}.


\fi

\section{Future Prospects: Spatiotemporal Digital Twins}
\label{sec:future}
\ifsubmission
\emph{A gap driven agenda.} Each direction below follows from a gap observed in the studies synthesized here, and the counts refer to those studies. \emph{Measurement}: one of the twelve studies in Table~\ref{tab:evidence} reports a model cost figure and none reports latency, data rate, bandwidth, or update strategy. The same task should therefore be benchmarked across device, gateway, and server placement, with parameters, latency, and energy reported beside accuracy. \emph{Fusion}: no study reports performance with each modality withheld. Leave-one-modality-out evaluation at measured device failure rates is needed before a modality is judged to earn its cost. \emph{Explanation}: attention maps are offered as interpretability without faithfulness testing, which perturbation of the attended region against matched random regions would supply. \emph{Federation}: results are reported as single accuracy figures, without the rounds, bandwidth, energy, privacy budget, and local-only baseline that determine feasibility. \emph{World model twins}: no reviewed system meets both the action conditioning and synchronization criteria and has been evaluated on outcomes. Target trial emulation followed by silent mode deployment is the natural first test. \emph{Lifestyle}, \emph{harmonization} and \emph{safety} complete the list. Our search surfaced no quantitative dietary study; cross-site variation attributable to harmonization choices is unmeasured; and no study evaluates sensor spoofing, action label tampering or planner manipulation for a twin permitted to act.

\emph{Networking directions.} The agenda assumes a communication substrate this literature leaves unspecified. The reviewed pipelines are described without a transport, yet the choice among publish--subscribe messaging for periodic vital summaries, low energy links for the body area tier, and low power wide area bearers for ambient nodes determines the achievable sampling rate and whether a model update can be pushed at all. Resource accounting should span model to deployment as one chain---bytes per patient-day, bytes after on-device summarization, model bytes per federated round, latency at each placement and energy at each hop---so a model choice is checked against a link budget rather than justified by accuracy. Reproducible multi-site testbeds with an edge tier of controllable bandwidth, latency and loss, plus released traces, would let a reported edge deployment be replicated rather than asserted.
\else
As DNNs advance, spatiotemporal modeling will increasingly integrate multimodal sources, improve efficiency through parallel computing, enhance interpretability, and adopt secure learning. We outline key directions below.
\paragraph{The Value of Digital Twin and Spatiotemporal Modeling in Digital Health}

Healthcare faces fragmented data, delayed diagnosis, limited personalization, and inefficient resource use, with scarce continuous high-quality data limiting AI. Digital twins with spatiotemporal modeling address this by maintaining real-time virtual patient representations updated from wearables, records, environmental sensors, and mobility, from which spatiotemporal models simulate trends, forecast progression, and support decisions while generating high-resolution, temporally aligned data~\cite{ali2024spatialtwins}. Wider adoption should accumulate diverse datasets and enable standardized cross-population models~\cite{bilal2025cityseircast}.

\paragraph{Exploration of Spatiotemporal Models and Technological Advancements}

Across dietary, diabetes, depression, and cardiac applications, deep learning consistently captures spatiotemporal dependencies and integrates multimodal information, aided by big data, cloud, and parallel computing. Key challenges remain: limited data availability and quality, privacy and cross-center collaboration, underexplored multimodal fusion, and real-time and generalization constraints. Privacy preserving methods such as federated learning and differential privacy are therefore essential, and as wearables, self-supervised learning, physics informed constraints, and edge computing mature, clinical adoption will require standardized protocols, robust data protection, reliable models, and tight workflow integration.
