\paragraph{Networking directions} The agenda above assumes a communication substrate that this literature has not specified, and closing that gap is a distinct line of work. Three questions are concrete enough to attack now. First, \emph{protocol fit}: the reviewed pipelines are described without a transport, yet the choice among MQTT or CoAP publish--subscribe messaging for periodic vital summaries, BLE and its gateway profile for the body area tier, and low power wide area bearers for ambient exposure nodes determines the achievable sampling rate, the tolerable payload, and whether an update can be pushed at all; profiles that carry model updates and provenance metadata alongside measurements, rather than measurements alone, do not yet exist for health twins. Second, \emph{a resource budget that spans model to deployment}: the quantities named above should be reported as one chain---bytes generated per patient-day at the sensor, bytes transmitted after on-device summarization, model bytes per federated round, inference latency at each candidate placement, and energy at each hop---so that a model choice can be checked against a link budget rather than justified only by accuracy. Third, \emph{shared testbeds}: progress on the questions above requires reproducible multi-site setups combining instrumented participants, an edge tier with controllable bandwidth, latency, and loss, and a federated coordinator, together with released traces so that a claimed edge deployment can be replicated rather than asserted. We regard open, characterized testbeds and pipeline cards as the most immediately actionable contribution the IoT community could make to this area.

\paragraph{Promoting Data Sharing in Digital Health Research}
Digital health integrates heterogeneous data from hospitals, wearables, environmental sensors, and genomics, so standardized collection, annotation, and exchange protocols are essential. Federated learning and secure multi-party computation let institutions retain data autonomy while collaborating, but only joint technical and legal progress can overcome data silos for precise monitoring and intervention.

\paragraph{From Clinical Trials to Real World Commercial Applications}
Translating these technologies to market requires stability and scalability in dynamic settings, balancing accuracy with cost, security, and user experience, and moving from controlled trials to population scale use through collaboration with insurers, pharma, and hospitals, with continuous iteration on user feedback.

\paragraph{Future World Model Based Digital Twins}
Future systems will likely benefit most from constrained world models synchronized through digital twins and informed by spatiotemporal context, compressing multimodal data into latent health states, updating continually, and rolling forward under candidate interventions. The challenge is combining the adaptability of world models with the personalization, synchronization, and accountability of medical twins, requiring hybrid mechanistic learning, multiscale organ--person--environment representations, privacy preserving continual learning, and prospective validation before deployment~\cite{DeDomenico2025ComplexSystems}.
\fi

