\section{Review Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}
\ifsubmission
The purposive, single-reviewer selection of Section~\ref{sec:method} is this review's principal limitation: the verification search there puts the eligible literature near 260 studies against the 12 we synthesize, so no statement about completeness or prevalence follows from any number we report, and the chosen sources exclude clinical registries, regulatory submissions, unpublished industrial deployments, non-English work and pre-2015 statistical literature. Part of the world model literature exists only as preprints and the published record over-represents positive results, so the figures in Table~\ref{tab:evidence} are likely optimistic; the studies also differ in cohort, outcome, horizon and validation split, so we report their numbers as stated and do not pool them. Assignment to layers, data types and feasible inference placement required judgment, and because the primary studies rarely report model size, latency, bandwidth or energy, our treatment of deployment characterizes an absence in the literature rather than measuring systems.
\else
Several limitations constrain how the preceding synthesis should be read.

\emph{Selection was narrative, not systematic.} Studies were chosen purposively to span data types, model families, and application domains, as described in Section~\ref{sec:method}. We did not pre-register a protocol, did not screen in duplicate, and did not record the number of records identified, screened, or excluded at each stage. No completeness or prevalence claim should be drawn from any count we report; counts describe the studies assembled here and nothing wider.

\emph{Search constraints.} We drew primarily on IEEE Xplore, PubMed, and Scopus, supplemented by citation chasing. Venues indexed elsewhere, in particular clinical trial registries, regulatory submissions, and industrial deployments that are never published, are systematically absent. Our window of roughly 2015--2026 excludes earlier statistical spatiotemporal work that remains relevant, and we restricted attention to English language publications, so evidence reported in other languages is missing.

\emph{Preprints and publication bias.} Part of the world model literature exists only as preprints and has not been peer reviewed; we cite such work as a statement of direction rather than as evidence, and label it accordingly in Section~\ref{sec:world_model_dt}. The published record also over-represents positive results, so the performance figures in Table~\ref{tab:evidence} are likely optimistic relative to what these methods achieve in routine use.

\emph{Heterogeneity prevents metric comparison.} The studies differ in cohort, outcome, horizon, preprocessing and validation split, and several rely on small or private cohorts. We therefore report each study's numbers as stated and deliberately do not pool, rank, or meta-analyze them; a higher accuracy in one row of Table~\ref{tab:evidence} does not mean a better model than a lower accuracy in another.

\emph{Taxonomy involves judgment.} Assignment of studies to data types, model families, unit of analysis, and inference placement required interpretation, particularly for multimodal work and for our judgment of where a study \emph{could} feasibly run rather than where it was run. A different reviewer could code some studies differently.

\emph{Missing system reporting limits the deployment analysis.} Because the primary studies rarely report model size, latency, data rate, bandwidth, energy, or update strategy, our treatment of deployment characterizes an absence in the literature rather than measuring the systems themselves. We did not re-implement or benchmark any model.

\emph{The world model literature is moving quickly.} That section is a conceptual synthesis of a field whose primary sources are appearing faster than they can be evaluated, and its assessment of the evidence is likely to date sooner than the rest of this survey.

\fi

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}
\ifsubmission
We answer the four questions in turn without introducing anything not supported above. The five data types overlap, and the sensing tier fixes data type, sampling rate and feasible inference location together (Fig.~\ref{fig:taxonomy}). Attention based and hybrid architectures generally reported gains once follow-up was incorporated dynamically, but the evidence in Table~\ref{tab:evidence} is not comparable across studies, our selection was purposive, no quantitative dietary study met the criteria, and deployment characteristics are almost never reported. Twins require multisource fusion, continual synchronization, privacy preserving training and an actionable interface, and current work meets these unevenly: fusion is evaluated on complete cases, federated results omit the quantities that determine feasibility, and visualization work largely evaluates rendering rather than synchronization. A world model twin is distinguished by an action conditioned transition and by synchronization with a return path to the individual, and we found no health system meeting both conditions evaluated against patient outcomes.
\else
We answer in turn the four research questions posed in the introduction, without introducing claims not already supported above.

\emph{RQ1.} Spatiotemporal data in connected digital health fall into five types---event, trajectory, point reference, raster, and video---overlaid by a relational layer, and these are not independent of the deployment that produces them: as set out in Section~\ref{sec:data}, the sensing tier fixes the data type, the achievable sampling rate, and the feasible location of inference together. The categories overlap, and we coded multimodal studies by the representation their model consumes.

\emph{RQ2.} Across the dietary, diabetes, depression, cardiac, and longitudinal studies synthesized in Table~\ref{tab:evidence}, attention based and hybrid spatial--temporal architectures generally reported gains over recurrent, convolutional, and classical baselines once follow-up data were incorporated dynamically. The evidence is not comparable across studies: cohorts, outcomes, horizons, and splits differ, several cohorts are small or private, and our selection was purposive. We found no quantitative dietary or lifestyle study meeting our stated criteria, and studies report predictive accuracy almost to the exclusion of deployment characteristics.

\emph{RQ3.} A medical digital twin requires multisource fusion, temporal alignment, continual synchronization to one identified individual, privacy preserving training, and an interface a clinician can act on. Current work meets these unevenly: fusion is evaluated on complete cases rather than under realistic modality loss, federated results are reported as single accuracy figures without the system quantities that determine feasibility, visualization work largely evaluates rendering rather than synchronization, and post-hoc attribution is offered where faithfulness testing and an explicit account of human intervention would be needed.

\emph{RQ4.} By the component criteria set out in Section~\ref{sec:world_model_dt}, a world model based twin is distinguished from a state-space model by an action conditioned transition and from a digital shadow by synchronization with a return path to the individual. On that definition we found no health system meeting both conditions that has been evaluated against patient outcomes. Section~\ref{sec:world_model_dt} is therefore a conceptual framework and research agenda rather than a synthesis of evaluated systems, and the directions in Section~\ref{sec:future} follow from the gaps this review observed rather than from expectation.

\fi

